{"reason": "The execution results show validation logloss stagnating around 0.51-0.53 with overfitting (train loss dropping to 0.45 while val loss increases). The initial plan identifies two root causes: the coarse gating mechanism (MultiHeadGating averages importance across authors) and under-regularization. Historical successful experiments confirm that per-author attention mechanisms and stronger regularization like stochastic depth and token-level dropout directly address overfitting and improve author-specific feature learning. A failed experiment using OneCycleLR with high dropout and LayerNorm did not improve performance because it reduced model capacity without addressing the author-specific attention bottleneck, thus I avoid that approach. The chosen modules—model_design for per-author attention and training_evaluation for enhanced regularization—are the most promising because they directly target the identified root causes and are validated by historical successes.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current MultiHeadGating module with PerAuthorAttention that uses three learnable query vectors (one per author: EAP, HPL, MWS) to attend over the fused 256-dimensional representation. Specifically, define query parameters of shape (3, 256) for keys derived from the fused multimodal output, compute attention scores via dot product, apply softmax to get per-author weights, and produce three author-specific context vectors. These are then linearly projected to class logits. This change directly addresses the initial plan's critique that averaging gating weights loses author-specific patterns. Implementation: modify the CrossAttentionFusion class to include a PerAuthorAttention layer with nn.Parameter for queries, and adjust the forward method to output three context vectors that feed into the classifier. Interface constraints: MultiInputClassifier's forward signature (tfidf, stylo, char_ids) remains unchanged.", "training_evaluation": "Add stochastic depth (drop-path) with a survival rate of 0.9 to the tfidf_branch and stylo_branch's Sequential blocks, and introduce token-level dropout of 0.05 in the CharCNN's embedding layer. Implement stochastic depth via a custom module that randomly drops entire branch activations during training. Token-level dropout masks random token positions in the character embedding output. These regularizations, inspired by historical successful experiments, prevent overfitting by forcing the model to rely on all modalities equally and learn robust character representations. Implementation: modify the forward passes of the branches and CharCNN embedding to apply stochastic depth and token-level dropout conditionally (train mode only). Interface constraints: MultiInputClassifier forward and CharCNN signatures remain unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show validation logloss stagnating around 0.51-0.53 with overfitting (train loss dropping to 0.45 while val loss increases). The initial plan identifies two root causes: the coarse gating mechanism (MultiHeadGating averages importance across authors) and under-regularization. Historical successful experiments confirm that per-author attention mechanisms and stronger regularization like stochastic depth and token-level dropout directly address overfitting and improve author-specific feature learning. A failed experiment using OneCycleLR with high dropout and LayerNorm did not improve performance because it reduced model capacity without addressing the author-specific attention bottleneck, thus I avoid that approach. The chosen modules—model_design for per-author attention and training_evaluation for enhanced regularization—are the most promising because they directly target the identified root causes and are validated by historical successes.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current MultiHeadGating module with PerAuthorAttention that uses three learnable query vectors (one per author: EAP, HPL, MWS) to attend over the fused 256-dimensional representation. Specifically, define query parameters of shape (3, 256) for keys derived from the fused multimodal output, compute attention scores via dot product, apply softmax to get per-author weights, and produce three author-specific context vectors. These are then linearly projected to class logits. This change directly addresses the initial plan's critique that averaging gating weights loses author-specific patterns. Implementation: modify the CrossAttentionFusion class to include a PerAuthorAttention layer with nn.Parameter for queries, and adjust the forward method to output three context vectors that feed into the classifier. Interface constraints: MultiInputClassifier's forward signature (tfidf, stylo, char_ids) remains unchanged.",
    "training_evaluation": "Add stochastic depth (drop-path) with a survival rate of 0.9 to the tfidf_branch and stylo_branch's Sequential blocks, and introduce token-level dropout of 0.05 in the CharCNN's embedding layer. Implement stochastic depth via a custom module that randomly drops entire branch activations during training. Token-level dropout masks random token positions in the character embedding output. These regularizations, inspired by historical successful experiments, prevent overfitting by forcing the model to rely on all modalities equally and learn robust character representations. Implementation: modify the forward passes of the branches and CharCNN embedding to apply stochastic depth and token-level dropout conditionally (train mode only). Interface constraints: MultiInputClassifier forward and CharCNN signatures remain unchanged."
  }
}"}

