{"reason": "The current execution results show a validation logloss plateau at ~0.3478 with clear overfitting (train accuracy reaches 99.9% while val accuracy caps at 88%). The initial plan proposes a multi-view ensemble using BERT as a feature extractor, TF-IDF/n-gram features, and stacked generalization. Historical successful experiments confirm that cross-attention fusion and differential learning rates with warmup effectively combat overfitting and improve generalization. A failed experiment that only increased regularization without architectural changes (using OneCycleLR with high dropout) did not help, so I avoid that. Therefore, I choose model_design to implement cross-attention fusion as a pragmatic step toward the ensemble vision, and training_evaluation to add warmup and differential learning rates to stabilize training. These two modules address the root cause (overfitting due to simple model fusion) and are directly informed by successful historical patterns.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current simple addition fusion of BERT pooled output and stylometric features with a cross-attention mechanism. Specifically, add a CrossAttentionFusion module where the BERT pooled output serves as query and the stylometric features (projected to same dimension) serve as keys/values, followed by a gated residual connection. This forces the model to learn joint contextual-stylistic patterns (e.g., long sentences with archaic vocabulary) rather than independent features, directly addressing the overfitting pattern. Implementation: create a CrossAttentionFusion class with multi-head attention (num_heads=4, d_model=768) and a gating mechanism, modify the forward method to use it. Interface constraints: ModernBERTClassifier class signature and forward method (input_ids, attention_mask, stylo_features) must remain unchanged; only internal architecture is modified.", "training_evaluation": "Add a linear learning rate warmup for 5 epochs before cosine annealing, and implement differential learning rates: set lr=1e-5 for the BERT backbone (slower to preserve pre-trained features) and lr=1e-4 for the new fusion and classification layers (faster adaptation). Increase total epochs to 25 with patience increased to 7. This stabilizes the gradients for the new attention mechanism, which has more parameters and can be unstable early in training, and follows the successful historical pattern of warmup + differential LR. Implementation: modify the optimizer to use separate parameter groups, and adjust the scheduler to use a custom LambdaLR with linear warmup followed by cosine decay. Interface constraints: train_model function signature must remain unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
